<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 8: Handling Missing Values                               -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Opens with a real, verified story: Abraham Wald and the           -->
<!--  Statistical Research Group's WWII aircraft-survivability work.    -->
<!--  Chosen deliberately over a "disaster" story (unlike ch2/ch4):     -->
<!--  it is the classic case of reasoning correctly, and it is exactly  -->
<!--  about the imputation/deletion-adjacent question this chapter      -->
<!--  needs (why is data missing, and what does that imply about how    -->
<!--  to treat it), which none of the ch4 stories (inconsistency,       -->
<!--  duplication, silent recoding) actually covered.                  -->
<!--  Verified via multiple independent searches before writing:       -->
<!--    - Wald, A. (1943). "A Method of Estimating Plane Vulnerability  -->
<!--      Based on Damage of Survivors." Statistical Research Group,    -->
<!--      Columbia University, memorandum for the National Defense     -->
<!--      Research Committee (reprinted, Center for Naval Analyses,    -->
<!--      1980).                                                       -->
<!--    - Mangel, M. and Samaniego, F.J. (1984). "Abraham Wald's Work   -->
<!--      on Aircraft Survivability." Journal of the American           -->
<!--      Statistical Association, 79(386), 259-267. Peer-reviewed      -->
<!--      formalisation of Wald's original (classified at the time)     -->
<!--      memoranda - used as the authoritative secondary source for    -->
<!--      what his method actually did technically, specifically to    -->
<!--      avoid the oversimplified pop-science "just armour the         -->
<!--      engines" version. His actual method: assume hits are roughly  -->
<!--      uniformly distributed over the airframe by surface area, then -->
<!--      use returning-plane damage counts to infer conditional        -->
<!--      survival/vulnerability by section, explicitly correcting for  -->
<!--      the missing (non-returning) planes rather than reading        -->
<!--      returning-plane damage at face value.                        -->
<!--  MCAR/MAR/MNAR formal definitions and the na.rm/complete.cases     -->
<!--  material are adapted from the old, unbuilt drafts                -->
<!--  (ch6_descriptive_statistics.ptx sec-missing-data, and             -->
<!--  ch6_description_2.ptx subsec-missing-formal), rewritten to open   -->
<!--  with Wald as the MNAR example rather than presenting the taxonomy -->
<!--  cold.                                                             -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-handling-missing-values" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Handling Missing Values</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-handling-missing-values">
    <p>
      In <xref ref="ch-exploring-data"/>, we ran into <c>NA</c> as soon as we
      tried to calculate a mean, and set the problem aside.
      In <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, we looked at the gaps, and started
      to think informally about why there were <c>NA</c> values in the data.
    </p>

    <p>
      The big idea in this chapter is easy to say, and easy to underestimate:
      <em>why</em> a value is missing decides what you should do about it.
      Sometimes missing values are just background noise, and you can safely
      ignore them.
      Sometimes they're the most important information in the whole dataset.
      The study given in <xref ref="sec-intro-missing-values"/> is one where
      working out that difference really mattered.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: Wald and the bombers                                  -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-intro-missing-values">
    <title>The Patients Who Aren't in the Data</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Adhiambo et al.
        (2025) followed adults getting HIV treatment at five health facilities
        in western Kenya, and asked a question that sounds simple: how many of
        them had missed a routine blood test they were supposed to have?
        The answer turned out to depend completely on working out <em>why</em>
        each person was missing, and not just how many were.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-background">
      <title>A test that tells you if treatment is working</title>

      <p>
        People living with HIV take antiretroviral therapy (ART) to keep the
        virus suppressed, ideally to levels so low that it can't be transmitted
        and can't seriously damage the body.
        The most direct way to know whether that's actually happening for a
        given patient is a <term>viral load</term> test: a blood test that
        measures how much virus is circulating.
        <fn>
    It isn't the only way. Before viral load testing became routine, clinics
    relied on CD4 cell counts (a proxy that tracks immune system damage) and
    on watching for clinical symptoms of disease progression. Both work, but
    both lag behind. Viral load can rise for months before CD4 count falls or
    symptoms appear, so a patient whose treatment is failing may go
    undetected for a long time under either older method. That's why viral
    load testing was treated as such a major improvement when it was
    introduced.
  </fn>
        Kenyan national guidelines, in line with World Health Organisation
        recommendations, say this test should happen 6 and 12 months after
        starting treatment, and every 12 months after that.
      </p>

      <p>
        The first year is when treatment failure is most likely, making timely
        viral load testing particularly important.
        Without these results, clinicians may miss early signs of treatment
        failure, allowing drug resistance to develop or the patient's health to
        deteriorate.
        A missed test could therefore mean that a patient is failing treatment
        without anyone knowing.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-study">
      <title>Following 1,754 people for two years</title>

      <p>
        The study sat inside a larger clinical trial running from 2014 to 2019,
        across three urban clinics in Kisumu County and two rural clinics in
        Migori County.
        Viral load monitoring wasn't managed specially for the trial, but
        happened through ordinary, routine clinic care.
        This means that the results show what <em>actually</em> happens in the
        health system, not what happens under ideal research conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        For every one of 1,754 participants, the researchers checked whether a
        viral load result existed in the 9 to 15 month window after starting
        treatment, and again in the 21 to 27 month window.
        If a result couldn't be found in the routine records, the researchers
        would check laboratory logs, a national database, paper files, and
        eventually contact clinicians directly, to work out, case by case, what
        had actually happened to that missing test.
        Later in this chapter, we'll come back to why that second step mattered
        so much.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-headline-number">
      <title>The number you'd report first</title>

      <p>
        In year one, 24.4% (428/1,754) of participants had no viral load result
        in their window.
        In year two, it was 29.4% (502/1,709)!
        Almost a third of patients, on the most basic reading of the data,
        didn't have the information their doctors needed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The temptation is to work with the data you have: Take the roughly 1,300
        people who do have a year-one viral load result, calculate whatever you
        need from them, and assume it applies to everyone.
      </p>

      <p>
        But, working with just the 1,300 who were tested is only safe if the
        reason <em>why</em> someone went untested has nothing to do with whether
        their treatment was working.
        In this dataset, it turns out that isn't the case.
      </p>

      <example>
        <p>
          Think about a class of 100 students sitting an exam, where 24 are
          absent on the day.
          If you calculate the class average from the 76 who showed up, is that
          a fair estimate of how the whole class of 100 would have done?
          It depends entirely on <em>why</em> those 24 weren't there.
          If they were kept home by 24 unrelated colds and stomach bugs, there's
          no reason to think they'd have scored any differently, so the average
          from the 76 is a perfectly good stand-in for all 100.
          But if most of the 24 stayed home because they hadn't done the reading
          and didn't want to sit the exam, the 76 who showed up are, on average,
          the more prepared half of the class.
          Their average isn't a fair estimate of everyone anymore.
          It's an estimate of the students who choose to turn up when they're
          ready.
        </p>
      </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-why">
      <title>So, why is it missing?</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          The extended investigation gave the missing results a reason, and the
          reasons were not evenly spread.
          <xref ref="table-missing-viral-load-reasons"/> summarises the reasons
          for missing viral load tests in the first year.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-missing-viral-load-reasons">
          <title>
            Reasons for missing viral load tests in year one, among 406
            participants who were still alive but had no recorded test result.
          </title>

          <tabular>
            <col halign="left"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <col halign="right"/>
            <row>
              <cell><em>Reason</em></cell>
              <cell><em>Number</em></cell>
              <cell><em>Percentage</em></cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Clinician misinterpreted the monitoring schedule</cell>
              <cell>149</cell>
              <cell>36.7%</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>
                Missed all clinic appointments during the testing window
              </cell>
              <cell>209</cell>
              <cell>51.5%</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>No documented reason</cell>
              <cell>42</cell>
              <cell>10.3%</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Test ordered, but no sample collection recorded</cell>
              <cell>6</cell>
              <cell>1.5%</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell><em>Total</em></cell>
              <cell>406</cell>
              <cell>100.0%</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Four reasons, and as we'll see, each one changes what the missing data
          is quietly telling you.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-vl-mcar">
        <title>Some results may be missing by chance</title>

        <p>
          There were six participants in year one whose clinician ordered a
          viral load test, but for whom no sample collection was recorded.
          One possible explanation is a simple administrative mistake.
          Perhaps the test was forgotten, or the paperwork was lost.
        </p>

        <p>
          Suppose that these mistakes happened entirely by chance, with no
          connection to the patients or how well their treatment was working.
        </p>

        <example>
          <p>
            Think back to our exam example.
            Imagine a student misses the exam because they catch a cold.
            Their illness has nothing to do with how well they prepared or what
            mark they would have received.
            If students missed the exam for reasons like this, completely
            independently of their marks and other characteristics, those who
            attended would still give us a reasonable picture of the class.
          </p>
        </example>

        <p>
          We would have fewer results to work with, so our estimate would be
          less precise.
          But we would not expect the missing students to be systematically
          better or worse than those who attended.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-vl-mar">
        <title>
          Some people are more likely to have missing data than others
        </title>

        <p>
          We have missing viral load results for some participants, but we still
          know other things about them, such as their age, sex and the clinic
          they attend.
          This information is recorded for everyone, whether or not they had a
          viral load test.
        </p>

        <p>
          When researchers looked at who was missing a viral load result, they
          found some important differences.
          Participants under 24 were more than twice as likely to have a missing
          result as participants over 44.
          In other words, if 10 out of every 100 older participants were missing
          a result, we would expect more than 20 out of every 100 younger
          participants to be missing one.
          Participants attending rural clinics were also more likely to have a
          missing result than those attending urban clinics.
        </p>

        <p>
          Why does this matter?
          Imagine we calculate the percentage of participants whose treatment is
          working, using only those with a viral load result.
          We now know that younger participants and those attending rural
          clinics are <em>less</em> likely to be included in our calculation.
          Our results will therefore tell us more about older participants and
          those attending urban clinics than about the whole group.
        </p>

        <p>
          This would not necessarily be a problem if everyone had similar
          treatment outcomes.
          But what if younger and older participants have different rates of
          viral suppression?
          Or rural and urban participants?
          We cannot simply assume that they are the same.
          The people missing from our calculation might have different results
          from those who are included.
        </p>

        <example>
          <p>
            Think back to our example of the exam.
            Imagine that the 24 absent students were mostly from a poorer part
            of town, further away from the school.
            The students who took the exam would then include fewer students
            from that part of town than the class as a whole.
            If students from different parts of town also tend to perform
            differently in exams, the average mark of those who attended might
            not give us a good picture of how the whole class would have
            performed.
          </p>

          <p>
            But suppose we already knew which part of town every student lived
            in, including those who missed the exam.
            We could compare students who live in the same area, rather than
            assuming that absent students would have performed like everyone who
            attended.
            This would give us a better starting point for estimating the
            missing marks.
          </p>
        </example>

        <p>
          We can do something similar with the viral load data to the example
          exam scenario.
          Because we know everyone's age and clinic location, we can compare
          like with like.
          Instead of assuming that an untested participant under 24 would have
          the same result as a tested participant over 44, we can look at the
          results of other tested participants under 24.
          Similarly, we can compare rural participants with other rural
          participants.
        </p>

        <p>
          This does not tell us exactly what the missing results would have
          been.
          Younger participants who missed their tests might still be different
          from younger participants who attended.
          But it gives us a more informed starting point than treating everyone
          as though they were the same.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection xml:id="ss-vl-mnar">
        <title>And some people are missing because they're sicker</title>

        <p>
          The study's analysis found that participants at the most advanced
          stage of HIV disease (WHO clinical stage 4), had more than twice the
          risk of missing their year-two viral load compared to those at stage
          1.
          The research team also learned something that never made it into any
          official record at all: some clinicians told them, when asked
          directly, that they had deliberately avoided sending viral load tests
          for patients they suspected were already failing treatment, because
          they already knew, or believed, the result would come back high.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is the most dangerous kind of missing data there is, because the
          reason for the gap is tangled up with the very thing you're trying to
          measure.
          Go back to the exam example.
          This is the student who stayed home because they hadn't done the
          reading and didn't want to sit a test they knew they'd fail.
          Their absence isn't unrelated to their score, like the cold, and it
          isn't something you could correct for with a note about which bus they
          take, like the traffic.
          Their absence <em>is</em> their score, in the sense that not showing
          up is itself evidence of how unprepared they were.
        </p>

        <p>
          The patients missing from the <q>viral load</q> column aren't a random
          slice of everyone, and they aren't a slice you can identify from
          something else in their file, the way you could identify an under-24
          or a rural patient.
          They're disproportionately the patients doing worst, and the very fact
          of their disease progressing is bound up with why they weren't tested.
          If you calculate what fraction of tested patients are virally
          suppressed and treat that as the answer for everyone, you'll come out
          too optimistic, because the sickest people were the ones most likely
          never to be tested in the first place.
          There's no group to compare like with like here, the way there was for
          age or clinic location.
          The information you'd need to correct for the gap is the same
          information that's missing.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <conclusion xml:id="ss-missingness-comparison">
        <p>
          We have now seen three different reasons why data might be missing.
          But how much difference can the reason for missingness actually make?
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's return to our exam example and imagine a class of 100 students.
          There are 70 students who live close to school and 30 who live further
          away.
          In our simulated class, students living closer to school tend to get
          higher marks than those living further away.
        </p>

        <p>
          Now suppose that 24 students miss the exam.
          We will look at three different situations, each with exactly 24
          students absent.
          The class and their potential exam marks stay exactly the same.
          The only thing we change is <em>who misses the exam</em>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-exam-missingness-comparison">
          <caption>
            How different reasons for missing data can change the exam results
            we observe.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/exam-missingness-comparison.png" width="100%">
            <description>
              <p>
                Four histograms show the exam marks from the same simulated
                class of 100 students.
                The first shows all 100 marks.
                The next three show the marks of 76 students, with 24 results
                missing in each case.
                In one, students are absent at random.
                In another, students living further away, who tend to have lower
                marks, are more likely to be absent.
                In the fourth panel, the students with the lowest marks are
                absent.
                A dashed vertical line shows the average of the available marks
                in each panel.
                The average is generally higher in the third and fourth examples
                because fewer low marks are included.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Look at <xref ref="fig-exam-missingness-comparison"/>.
          In every situation, we are working with the same class of students.
          And in each of the three missing-data examples, we have exactly 76
          exam results.
        </p>

        <p>
          In the first missing-data example, students are absent completely at
          random.
          Some students with high marks are missing, and some with low marks are
          missing.
          We lose information, but there is no systematic reason for the average
          to move upwards or downwards.
        </p>

        <p>
          In the second example, most of the absent students live further away
          from school.
          Because students from that area tend to get lower marks in our
          simulated class, the average of the students who took the exam is
          higher than the average for the whole class.
        </p>

        <p>
          However, we know where every student lives, including those who were
          absent.
          This gives us useful information.
          We can compare students from the same area, rather than treating
          everyone as though they were the same.
        </p>

        <p>
          In the third example, the students with the lowest marks are the ones
          who miss the exam.
          The average mark of those who attended is now much higher than the
          average for the whole class.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is particularly difficult because the missing marks themselves
          are connected to why the students were absent.
          Even if we know where each student lives, the students who missed the
          exam may have performed worse than the students who attended from the
          same area.
        </p>

        <p>
          Notice that the number of missing values was the same in all three
          examples.
          What changed was which values were missing.
          This is why, when investigating missing data, we need to ask more than
          just <q>How many values are missing?</q> We also need to ask
          <q>Why are they missing?</q>
        </p>
      </conclusion>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-vl-lesson">
      <title>Why the reason for missing data matters</title>

      <p>
        We started with viral load results with 24.4% missing.
        But as we investigated further, we found that those missing results had
        very different explanations.
      </p>

      <p>
        More than half of the missing results were from patients who had stopped
        attending clinic appointments.
        Some might have transferred to another clinic, others might have stopped
        receiving care altogether, and some might have died without this being
        recorded <ndash/> we cannot tell from the missing result alone.
      </p>

      <p>
        More than a third were linked to clinicians misunderstanding the
        monitoring schedule.
        A small number had a test ordered but no record of a sample being
        collected.
        These could reflect administrative problems rather than anything about
        the patients' health.
      </p>

      <p>
        But we also saw patterns <ndash/> younger patients, patients attending
        rural clinics, and patients with more advanced HIV disease were more
        likely to have missing results.
        Some clinicians even reported avoiding tests when they already suspected
        that a patient's treatment was failing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Think back to our exam example.
        We had the same class of students, with the same 24 missing exam marks,
        but the average we calculated could change depending on which students
        were absent.
        Missing 24 marks by chance was very different from missing 24 marks
        mostly from students who expected to fail.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same applies here.
        If patients whose treatment is failing are more likely to have missing
        viral load results, calculating the percentage with viral suppression
        using only the patients who were tested could give us a misleadingly
        optimistic picture.
      </p>

      <p>
        Adhiambo et al.
        (2025) recognised this problem.
        They concluded that missing viral load results could substantially
        change estimates of viral suppression.
        The research team also reported using laboratory data for routine
        reporting rather than relying on the case database alone.
      </p>

      <p>
        We need to understand <em>why</em> a value is missing to be able to
        handle it responsibly.
        It may be unrelated to the results we are interested in, or it may be
        explained (at least partly) by information we already have.
        But, sometimes the missing result itself may be connected to why it is
        missing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each situation has different consequences for our analysis, and removing
        rows with missing values might be reasonable in some circumstances, but
        misleading in others.
        Filling in missing values also requires assumptions about what those
        values might have been.
      </p>

      <p>
        We do not always have enough information to know exactly why data is
        missing, or to recover the missing values, but that does not mean we
        should ignore the problem.
      </p>

      <warning>
        <title>Ignoring missing data is still a decision</title>

        <p>
          When you analyse only the values you have, you are making a decision
          about how to handle the missing ones, even if you do not realise it.
        </p>

        <p>
          That decision may have little effect, or it may completely change your
          conclusions.
          Before ignoring missing values, ask yourself:
          <em>Why might these values be missing, and could that affect my results?</em>
        </p>
      </warning>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Adhiambo, H.F., Lewis-Kulzer, J., Nyagesoa, E.,
        Gimbel, S., Akama, E., Mangale, D., Montonya, L., Eshun-Wilson, I.,
        Iguna, S., Nyandieka, E., Bukusi, E., Abuogi, L., Odeny, T., Petersen,
        M.L.
        and Geng, E.H.
        (2025).
        <q>Examining and classifying reasons for missing viral
    loads among adults living with HIV: An extended
    outcome investigation and ascertainment approach
    in Western Kenya.</q>
        <em>PLOS Global Public Health</em>, 5(5), e0004038.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch8-stack">
    <title>Practise: Handling Missing Values</title>

    <exercise>
      <title>Handling Missing Values</title>

      <stack source="stack/Statistics-and-Probability-Open-Question-Bank/top/STACK-for-Statistics-and-Probability/missing-values-1.xml" label="stk-ch8-handling-nas"/>
    </exercise>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: applied to the africa_indicators data                 -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-missing-data-r">
    <title>Missing Data in Practice: African Development Indicators</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The viral load study had a team who went and found out why each result
        was missing.
        In practice, we usually have the dataset and nothing else, so we have to
        work out what we can from the data itself.
        This section does that, using the <c>africa_indicators</c> dataset in
        the <c>statsdata</c> package.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <c>africa_indicators</c> data holds World Bank development
        indicators for the 54 African countries, one row per country per year
        from 2010 to 2023.
        There are 756 rows, with population, GDP per capita, life expectancy,
        under-5 mortality, HIV prevalence, births attended by skilled health
        staff, primary school enrolment, and the poverty rate.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        library(statsdata)
        library(dplyr)
        library(ggplot2)
        library(naniar)

        data(africa_indicators)
        </code>
      </program>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-missing-finding">
      <title>Finding missing values</title>

      <p>
        Before we can decide what to do about missing values, we need to know
        where they are.
        Are there just a few gaps, or are large parts of the dataset missing?
      </p>

      <p>
        In R, <c>is.na()</c> tells us whether a value is missing.
        It returns <c>TRUE</c> if the value is missing, and <c>FALSE</c> if it
        is present.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let's start by looking at the first six values of <c>births_skilled</c>,
        which records the percentage of births attended by skilled health
        personnel.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
head(is.na(africa_indicators$births_skilled))
#&gt; [1] TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Five of these six values are missing.
        But what about the whole column?
        We can use <c>sum()</c> to count the number of <c>TRUE</c> values.
        In R, <c>TRUE</c> is counted as 1 and <c>FALSE</c> as 0.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
sum(is.na(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment))
#&gt; [1] 219
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        There are 219 missing values in <c>primary_enrolment</c>.
        Since the dataset contains 756 rows, 29% of the country-year
        observations have no primary enrolment figure.
      </p>

      <p>
        We could repeat this calculation for every variable, but R can do that
        for us.
        <c>colSums()</c> adds up the missing values in each column.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
colSums(is.na(africa_indicators))
#&gt;              iso3           country            region      income_group
#&gt;                 0                 0                 0                 0
#&gt;              year        population    gdp_per_capita   life_expectancy
#&gt;                 0                 0                20                 0
#&gt;  under5_mortality    hiv_prevalence    births_skilled primary_enrolment
#&gt;                 0                56               583               219
#&gt;      poverty_rate
#&gt;               634
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Five of the thirteen columns have missing values.
        But the amount missing varies enormously.
        <c>gdp_per_capita</c> has just 20 missing values, fewer than 3% of its
        observations, while <c>poverty_rate</c> has 634, around 84%.
      </p>

      <p>
        Counting missing values is useful, but it doesn't tell us whether they
        occur together, or whether they follow any particular pattern.
        For that, a graph can help.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <c>naniar</c> package includes a function called <c>vis_miss()</c>,
        which shows where values are missing across the dataset.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
library(naniar)

vis_miss(africa_indicators)
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-africa-vis-miss">
        <caption>
          Missing values in the <c>africa_indicators</c> dataset.
          Each row represents a country in a particular year, and each column
          represents a variable.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch-missing-vis-miss.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A heatmap with one row per country-year and one column per
              variable.
              Most columns contain no missing values.
              The births_skilled and poverty_rate columns are mostly missing,
              primary_enrolment has scattered gaps, and gdp_per_capita and
              hiv_prevalence have blocks of missing values.
              The percentage missing is displayed for each variable.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Look at <xref ref="fig-africa-vis-miss"/>.
        Some columns are almost entirely missing, while others have just a few
        gaps.
        Notice the blocks of missing values in <c>gdp_per_capita</c> and
        <c>hiv_prevalence</c>.
        These are worth investigating.
        Could they correspond to particular countries or years?
      </p>

      <p>
        Another useful function is <c>gg_miss_var()</c>, which produces a bar
        chart showing the number of missing values in each variable.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
gg_miss_var(africa_indicators)
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-africa-gg-miss-var">
        <caption>
          Number of missing values in each variable of the
          <c>africa_indicators</c> dataset.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch-missing-gg-miss-var.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A horizontal bar chart showing the number of missing values per
              variable.
              Poverty rate has the most, with 634, followed by births attended
              by skilled health personnel with 583, primary enrolment with 219,
              HIV prevalence with 56 and GDP per capita with 20.
              All other variables have none.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        We now know how much data is missing from each variable.
        But we also need to investigate <em>which</em> observations are missing.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-missing-patterns">
      <title>Patterns of missingness</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          We have found the missing values and counted them.
          Now let's look more closely at where they occur.
        </p>

        <p>
          Think back to our exam example.
          Knowing that 24 students were absent told us very little until we
          discovered which students they were.
          Were they absent by chance, or were particular groups more likely to
          be missing?
        </p>

        <p>
          We can ask similar questions about our African development indicators.
          Are entire countries missing?
          Are particular years missing?
          Do the observations with missing values differ from those with
          recorded values?
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Are entire countries missing?</title>

        <p>
          Let's start with <c>hiv_prevalence</c>.
          We know that 56 values are missing.
          But are these scattered across many countries, or concentrated in just
          a few?
        </p>

        <p>
          We can use <c>filter()</c> to select the rows where HIV prevalence is
          missing, and <c>count()</c> to see which countries they belong to.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  filter(is.na(hiv_prevalence)) %&gt;%
  count(country, income_group)

# A tibble: 4 x 3
  country                income_group           n
  &lt;chr&gt;                  &lt;fct&gt;              &lt;int&gt;
1 Equatorial Guinea      Upper middle income    14
2 Mauritius              Upper middle income    14
3 Sao Tome and Principe  Lower middle income    14
4 Seychelles             High income            14
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          All 56 missing values come from just four countries.
          For each of these countries, HIV prevalence is missing for all 14
          years.
          This is quite different from having a few missing observations
          scattered across the dataset.
        </p>

        <p>
          Does it matter which countries are missing?
          Think back to our exam example <ndash/> of the absent students came
          mostly from one part of town, the students who attended might not
          represent the whole class.
        </p>

        <p>
          Could something similar be happening here?
          Let's compare the four countries without HIV prevalence data with the
          countries that do have it.
        </p>

        <p>
          We cannot compare their HIV prevalence, since that is precisely what
          is missing.
          But we can compare other information that we do have, such as GDP per
          capita and under-5 mortality.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(hiv_missing = is.na(hiv_prevalence)) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    mean_under5 = mean(under5_mortality),
    mean_gdp = mean(gdp_per_capita, na.rm = TRUE),
    n = n()
  )

# A tibble: 2 x 4
  hiv_missing mean_under5 mean_gdp     n
  &lt;lgl&gt;             &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1 FALSE              69.7    2145.    700
2 TRUE               35.1    9430.     56
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          There is quite a difference!
          The countries without HIV prevalence data have, on average, about half
          the under-5 mortality and more than four times the GDP per capita of
          the countries with data.
        </p>

        <p>
          These are averages across country-years, rather than individual
          countries, but they show that the missing observations are different
          in other important ways.
        </p>

        <p>
          Why does this matter?
          Suppose we wanted to investigate the relationship between HIV
          prevalence and economic development across Africa.
          We would have no HIV prevalence figures for these four countries, so
          they could not contribute to that analysis.
          Our results would describe the countries with available data, which
          might not give us a complete picture of Africa.
        </p>

        <p>
          We have found a pattern in the missing data.
          But we still don't know <em>why</em> these four countries have no HIV
          prevalence figures.
          The pattern gives us a reason to investigate further, not a definite
          explanation.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Are particular years missing?</title>

        <p>
          Next, let's look at <c>gdp_per_capita</c>.
          There are only 20 missing values, fewer than 3% of the dataset.
        </p>

        <p>
          That might not sound like much.
          But before deciding that these missing values are unimportant, let's
          find out where they occur.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  filter(is.na(gdp_per_capita)) %&gt;%
  count(country)

# A tibble: 2 x 2
  country         n
  &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;int&gt;
1 Eritrea        12
2 South Sudan     8
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          All 20 missing values come from just two countries.
          Unlike the HIV prevalence example, however, these countries do have
          GDP data for some years.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's investigate which years are missing.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  filter(is.na(gdp_per_capita)) %&gt;%
  group_by(country) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    first_missing = min(year),
    last_missing = max(year),
    n_missing = n()
  )

# A tibble: 2 x 4
  country     first_missing last_missing n_missing
  &lt;chr&gt;               &lt;int&gt;        &lt;int&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 Eritrea              2012         2023        12
2 South Sudan          2016         2023         8
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Eritrea has no GDP per capita figures from 2012 onwards, while South
          Sudan has none from 2016 onwards.
          In both cases, the missing values form one long, uninterrupted period.
        </p>

        <p>
          What might explain this?
          GDP figures depend on national statistical systems collecting and
          compiling economic information.
          Conflict, political instability, difficulties collecting data, and
          delays in producing national accounts can all affect whether figures
          are available.
        </p>

        <p>
          Both Eritrea and South Sudan have faced substantial political and
          economic challenges.
          However, we cannot tell from this dataset alone exactly why their GDP
          figures are missing.
          We would need to investigate the original data source and its
          documentation.
        </p>

        <p>
          What we can say is that the gaps are concentrated in particular
          countries and years.
        </p>

        <p>
          If we removed every row without GDP per capita, we would lose most of
          Eritrea's observations and all of South Sudan's observations from 2016
          onwards.
        </p>

        <p>
          So even though fewer than 3% of the GDP values are missing, removing
          them could have a much bigger effect on an analysis of these
          particular countries.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Does missingness relate to other variables?</title>

        <p>
          So far, we have found missing values concentrated in particular
          countries and years.
          But could missingness also be related to other characteristics (that
          we have measured in our data!)?
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's investigate <c>primary_enrolment</c>.
          We know that 219 values are missing.
          First, we can compare the proportion missing in each income group.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(income_group) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    n_total = n(),
    n_missing = sum(is.na(primary_enrolment)),
    pct_missing = round(n_missing / n_total * 100, 1)
  )

# A tibble: 4 x 4
  income_group         n_total n_missing pct_missing
  &lt;fct&gt;                  &lt;int&gt;     &lt;int&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Low income               294        92        31.3
2 Lower middle income      336        81        24.1
3 Upper middle income      112        46        41.1
4 High income               14         0         0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The proportion missing varies considerably.
          Around 41% of upper-middle-income observations are missing, compared
          with 24% of lower-middle-income observations.
        </p>

        <p>
          The high-income group has no missing values, although this group
          contains only Seychelles, so we should be careful about drawing
          conclusions from it.
        </p>

        <p>
          What about time?
          Are some years more likely to have missing enrolment data than others?
          Let's calculate the percentage missing for each year and plot the
          results.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(year) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    pct_missing = mean(is.na(primary_enrolment)) * 100
  ) %&gt;%
  ggplot(aes(x = year, y = pct_missing)) +
  geom_col(fill = "#F37021") +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = 2010:2023) +
  labs(
    x = "Year",
    y = "Countries with no value (%)"
  ) +
  theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <figure xml:id="fig-africa-enrolment-year">
          <caption>
            Percentage of countries with missing primary enrolment figures, by
            year.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch-missing-enrolment-year.png" width="90%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A bar chart showing the percentage of countries missing primary
                enrolment data each year from 2010 to 2023.
                The percentage increases after 2015, reaching approximately 40%
                in several years from 2019 onwards.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Missing values become more common in the later years of the dataset.
          This could partly reflect delays in collecting, processing, or
          publishing education statistics.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is one more question worth asking.
          Are the country-years with missing enrolment data different from those
          with available data?
        </p>

        <p>
          We cannot compare their enrolment rates, because those are missing.
          But we can compare other information, such as under-5 mortality and
          life expectancy.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(
    enrolment_missing = is.na(primary_enrolment)
  ) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    mean_under5 = mean(under5_mortality),
    mean_life = mean(life_expectancy),
    n = n()
  )

# A tibble: 2 x 4
  enrolment_missing mean_under5 mean_life     n
  &lt;lgl&gt;                   &lt;dbl&gt;     &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1 FALSE                    63.7      62.9   537
2 TRUE                     75.6      61.6   219
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Country-years without enrolment data have higher under-5 mortality:
          75.6 deaths per 1,000 live births, compared with 63.7 where enrolment
          data is available.
          They also have slightly lower life expectancy.
        </p>

        <p>
          This does not mean that missing enrolment data causes higher child
          mortality, or that we know the missing enrolment rates are lower.
          But it does show that the observations with missing data differ in
          other important ways.
        </p>

        <p>
          Imagine that we wanted to study the relationship between primary
          school enrolment and child health.
        </p>

        <p>
          If we simply removed all the rows with missing enrolment, we would
          disproportionately exclude observations with higher child mortality.
          That could affect the conclusions we draw.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is another example of why comparing like with like matters.
          We already know that missingness differs by income group and year.
          We can use that information to investigate the gaps more carefully,
          rather than assuming that the observations with available enrolment
          data represent every country-year.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>What if the data was never collected?</title>

        <p>
          Finally, let's look at <c>poverty_rate</c> and <c>births_skilled</c>.
          Both have many missing values, but there may be a different
          explanation for these gaps.
        </p>

        <p>
          Consider Kenya's data.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  filter(country == "Kenya") %&gt;%
  select(year, births_skilled, poverty_rate)

# A tibble: 14 x 3
   year births_skilled poverty_rate
  &lt;int&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;
 1 2010              NA           NA
 2 2011              NA           NA
 3 2012              NA           NA
 4 2013              NA           NA
 5 2014            61.8           NA
 6 2015              NA         37.7
 7 2016            70.2           NA
 8 2017              NA           NA
 9 2018              NA           NA
10 2019              NA           NA
11 2020              NA         44.4
12 2021              NA         46.4
13 2022              NA         45.5
14 2023              NA           NA
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Kenya has just two values for births attended by skilled health
          personnel, and four for poverty, across 14 years.
        </p>

        <p>
          Why might this be?
          Unlike some indicators that are estimated annually, these measures
          often rely on household surveys.
          Large surveys are expensive and time-consuming, so they are generally
          conducted periodically rather than every year.
        </p>

        <p>
          For example, Kenya's Demographic and Health Surveys (DHS) collect
          information about maternal and child health, including assistance
          during childbirth.
          Poverty estimates also depend heavily on household survey information.
        </p>

        <p>
          A missing value in an annual dataset may mean that no new estimate was
          available for that year.
          We would need to check the original source documentation to establish
          which surveys or estimates produced the figures shown here.
        </p>

        <p>
          Importantly, we should not assume that a missing year has the same
          value as the previous year.
          Poverty and health indicators can change between surveys, even when we
          do not have measurements to show it.
        </p>

        <insight xml:id="insight-missing-not-random">
          <title>Missing values can tell us something</title>

          <p>
            In this investigation, we have seen several different patterns
            <ndash/> sometimes entire countries are missing, other times gaps
            are concentrated in particular years, and sometimes missingness is
            associated with other characteristics, such as income group or child
            mortality.
            And sometimes an annual value may be missing because the underlying
            data is only collected periodically.
          </p>

          <p>
            If we remove every row containing a missing value, we may also
            remove particular countries, years or groups from our analysis.
            The remaining data might give us a different picture from the one we
            intended to study.
          </p>

          <p>
            But remember that finding a pattern is not the same as knowing its
            cause.
            The data can show us <em>where</em> values are missing and how
            missingness relates to other information.
            To understand <em>why</em>, we may need to investigate further.
          </p>
        </insight>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-missing-options">
      <title>What should we do about missing values?</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          We have now investigated where values are missing in the
          <c>africa_indicators</c> dataset.
          We found that some gaps are concentrated in particular countries, some
          occur more often in certain years, and others are associated with
          characteristics such as income group.
        </p>

        <p>
          But what happens when we want to use the data?
          Should we remove the missing values?
          Leave them alone?
          Or try to fill them in?
        </p>

        <p>
          There is no single answer that works for every dataset.
          What we decide depends on which values are missing, why they might be
          missing, and what we are trying to find out.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>What happens if we ignore the missing values?</title>

        <p>
          Let's start with something simple.
          Suppose we want to calculate the average primary school enrolment rate
          across the observations in our dataset.
        </p>

        <p>
          We might try:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
mean(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment)

#&gt; [1] NA
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          R returns <c>NA</c>.
          Why?
        </p>

        <p>
          We have 756 observations, but 219 of them have no primary enrolment
          figure.
          R cannot calculate the average of all 756 values because some of those
          values are unknown.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can tell R to calculate the average using only the values that are
          available.
          We do this by adding <c>na.rm = TRUE</c>.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
mean(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment,
     na.rm = TRUE)

#&gt; [1] 102.4205
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Now we have an answer: approximately 102.4.
          But what exactly have we calculated?
        </p>

        <p>
          This is the average of the 537 country-years with recorded enrolment
          figures, not the average of all 756 country-years.
          We have left out 219 observations.
        </p>

        <p>
          Remember what we found earlier: Missing enrolment figures were more
          common in recent years and differed between income groups.
          This means that the observations included in our average may not
          represent all the country-years equally well.
        </p>

        <p>
          Adding <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> has not solved the missing-data problem.
          We are just ignoring the missing values (and possibly therefore
          ignoring the problem!).
          This might be reasonable, depending on our question.
          But we should understand what the resulting number represents.
        </p>

        <remark xml:id="remark-report-n">
          <title>How many observations were used?</title>

          <p>
            When reporting a summary, it is good practice to say how many
            observations were used to calculate it.
            We often call this number <m>n</m>.
          </p>

          <p>
            For example, reporting an average enrolment rate of 102.4 tells us
            less than reporting an average of 102.4 based on 537 country-years,
            with 219 missing observations.
          </p>
        </remark>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>What if we remove every incomplete row?</title>

        <p>
          So far, we have only left out missing values from one variable.
          But suppose we want to analyse several variables together.
        </p>

        <p>
          One approach is to remove any row containing a missing value.
          In R, <c>complete.cases()</c> identifies rows with no missing values.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
sum(complete.cases(africa_indicators))

#&gt; [1] 22

nrow(africa_indicators)

#&gt; [1] 756
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Only 22 of our 756 rows are complete!
          If we removed every row containing a missing value, we would lose more
          than 97% of the dataset.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is largely because <c>poverty_rate</c> and <c>births_skilled</c>
          are missing for most country-years.
        </p>

        <p>
          But do we always need those variables?
          Suppose our analysis is about GDP, life expectancy and HIV prevalence.
          Missing poverty figures would not prevent us from using the other
          information in that row.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's see what happens if we exclude <c>poverty_rate</c> and
          <c>births_skilled</c> before checking for complete rows.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  select(-poverty_rate, -births_skilled) %&gt;%
  complete.cases() %&gt;%
  sum()

#&gt; [1] 491
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Now we have 491 complete rows instead of 22.
          This is much better <ndash/> deciding which variables we need before
          removing incomplete observations has meant we have far more data
          points!
        </p>

        <p>
          However, these 491 rows are not necessarily representative of the
          original dataset.
          For example, the four countries with no HIV prevalence figures are
          still excluded.
        </p>

        <p>
          We should therefore ask not only how many rows remain, but also
          <em>which</em> rows remain.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Can we keep the incomplete rows?</title>

        <p>
          Removing rows is not always necessary.
          We can often keep them in our dataset and use the information that is
          available for each analysis.
        </p>

        <p>
          For example, suppose we want to compare primary school enrolment
          across income groups.
          We can calculate the average for each group, while also reporting how
          many observations were available and how many were missing.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(income_group) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    mean_enrolment = mean(primary_enrolment,
                          na.rm = TRUE),
    n = sum(!is.na(primary_enrolment)),
    n_missing = sum(is.na(primary_enrolment))
  )

# A tibble: 4 x 4
  income_group        mean_enrolment     n n_missing
  &lt;fct&gt;                        &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;int&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
1 Low income                    102.   202        92
2 Lower middle income           103.   255        81
3 Upper middle income           101.    66        46
4 High income                   115.    14         0
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          This table tells us more than the averages alone <ndash/> for
          upper-middle-income countries, the average enrolment rate is around
          101, but it is based on just 66 observations.
          Another 46 observations have missing values.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can compare the groups, but we should be careful about our
          conclusions.
          The averages describe the observations with available data in each
          group.
          They do not necessarily describe every country-year in that group.
        </p>

        <p>
          This connects to our earlier idea of comparing like with like.
          By looking at income groups separately, we can see how the amount of
          missing data differs between groups.
        </p>

        <p>
          But grouping the data does not automatically correct for missingness.
          For example, if more recent years are missing within an income group,
          the available observations may still give us a misleading picture.
        </p>

        <p>
          Keeping the incomplete rows and reporting the missing values allows us
          to be more transparent about what we know and what we do not know.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>What if we fill in the missing values?</title>

        <p>
          Another possibility is to replace missing values with estimates.
          This is called <term>imputation</term>.
        </p>

        <p>
          For example, we might replace every missing primary enrolment figure
          with the average of the values we do have.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let's try it.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
enrolment_imputed &lt;- ifelse(
  is.na(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment),
  mean(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment,
       na.rm = TRUE),
  africa_indicators$primary_enrolment
)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          We now have a value for every row.
          But have we really recovered the missing information?
        </p>

        <p>
          Not exactly.
          We have replaced 219 unknown values with 219 copies of the same
          number: approximately 102.4.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can see what happens to the distribution of primary school
          enrolment when we replace the missing values with the mean.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-enrolment-mean-imputation">
          <caption>
            Distribution of primary school enrolment before and after mean
            imputation.
            Orange represents values filled in using the observed mean, rather
            than values actually recorded in the dataset.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/enrolment-mean-imputation.png" width="95%">
            <description>
              <p>
                Two histograms show the distribution of primary school enrolment
                rates before and after mean imputation.
                The original distribution contains 537 observed values.
                After imputation, 219 additional values appear in a large orange
                bar near the mean of 102.4.
                The original observed values remain unchanged.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Look at the second graph.
          There is now a large concentration of values around 102.4.
          But these are not new observations.
          We have created them by replacing all 219 missing values with the same
          number.
        </p>

        <p>
          The dataset now looks more complete, but it also looks less variable.
          We have introduced an artificial concentration of values around the
          mean.
          This helps explain why the standard deviation decreased after
          imputation.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is one of the dangers of filling in missing values.
          The dataset may look more complete, but the new values are estimates,
          not measurements.
        </p>

        <p>
          We also have to think about whether the estimates are reasonable.
          Remember that enrolment figures were missing more often in certain
          years and income groups.
          Replacing all missing values with the same overall average ignores
          those differences.
        </p>

        <p>
          More sophisticated methods can use other information, such as year,
          country or income group, to help estimate missing values.
          But those methods also make assumptions, and they cannot guarantee
          that the estimates are correct.
        </p>

        <p>
          For now, the important lesson is that filling in a missing value does
          not make it an observed value.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>

      <subsubsection>
        <title>Which approach should we choose?</title>

        <p>
          We have seen several ways of dealing with missing values.
          We can calculate summaries using only the available observations,
          remove incomplete rows, keep incomplete records and report the gaps,
          or estimate missing values.
        </p>

        <p>
          None of these approaches is always the right one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Before choosing, we need to think about what we are trying to find
          out, how much data is missing, and whether the missing observations
          might differ from those we have.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-missing-options">
          <title>
            Some approaches to handling missing values, and questions to
            consider before using them.
          </title>

          <tabular>
            <col halign="left" width="25%"/>
            <col halign="left" width="35%"/>
            <col halign="left" width="40%"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Approach</cell>
              <cell>What it does</cell>
              <cell>What to consider</cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Use available values</cell>
              <cell>Calculates a summary using only the recorded values.</cell>
              <cell>
                Are the observations with available values representative of the
                group we want to describe?
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Remove incomplete rows</cell>
              <cell>
                Keeps only rows with values for the variables being used.
              </cell>
              <cell>
                How many rows are lost, and are particular countries, years or
                groups excluded?
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Keep incomplete records</cell>
              <cell>
                Retains the rows and uses available information where possible.
              </cell>
              <cell>
                Which analyses can still be carried out, and how will missing
                values be reported?
              </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
              <cell>Estimate missing values</cell>
              <cell>
                Replaces missing values with estimates based on assumptions or
                other information.
              </cell>
              <cell>
                How were the estimates made, and how might they affect the
                results?
              </cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          Notice that the first three approaches do not recover the missing
          information.
          They change how we use the information that is available.
        </p>

        <p>
          Estimating missing values goes a step further, but it introduces
          assumptions about what those values might have been.
        </p>

        <p>
          Whichever approach we choose, we should be clear about what we have
          done and why we chose to do it.
        </p>
      </subsubsection>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-handling-missing-values">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-africa-missing-investigation">
      <title>Investigating missing life and health data</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          We have investigated missing values in primary school enrolment, GDP
          per capita and HIV prevalence.
        </p>

        <p>
          Now investigate the missing values in <c>births_skilled</c>, the
          percentage of births attended by skilled health personnel.
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                How many values are missing from <c>births_skilled</c>?
                What percentage of the dataset does this represent?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Calculate the percentage of missing values for each income
                group.
                Are some groups more affected than others?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Investigate whether missingness changes over time.
                Create a graph showing the percentage of countries with missing
                values in each year.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Compare the average under-5 mortality for observations with and
                without <c>births_skilled</c> data.
                What do you notice?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Suppose you wanted to investigate the relationship between
                skilled birth attendance and child mortality.
                What might happen if you simply removed every observation with
                missing <c>births_skilled</c>?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          First, we can count how many values are missing from
          <c>births_skilled</c> and calculate the percentage missing.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
sum(is.na(africa_indicators$births_skilled))
#&gt; [1] 583

mean(is.na(africa_indicators$births_skilled)) * 100
#&gt; [1] 77.1164
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          There are 583 missing values, approximately 77.1% of the dataset.
          This means that fewer than one in four country-year observations have
          a recorded value.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can investigate whether the proportion missing differs between
          income groups.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(income_group) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    n_total = n(),
    n_missing = sum(is.na(births_skilled)),
    pct_missing = round(
      mean(is.na(births_skilled)) * 100, 1
    )
  )
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The resulting table shows how many values are missing in each income
          group.
          Compare the percentages rather than just the counts, since the groups
          contain different numbers of observations.
        </p>

        <p>
          Next, we can investigate whether missingness changes over time.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(year) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    pct_missing = mean(is.na(births_skilled)) * 100
  ) %&gt;%
  ggplot(aes(x = year, y = pct_missing)) +
  geom_col(fill = "#F47721") +
  labs(
    x = "Year",
    y = "Countries with missing values (%)"
  ) +
  theme_bw()
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The graph allows us to see whether missing values are concentrated in
          particular years.
          Because skilled birth attendance figures often come from periodic
          surveys, we might expect gaps between years with available estimates.
          However, we would need to check the data source to establish why
          particular years are missing.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can also compare under-5 mortality for observations with and
          without skilled birth attendance data.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
africa_indicators %&gt;%
  group_by(
    births_missing = is.na(births_skilled)
  ) %&gt;%
  summarise(
    mean_under5 = mean(under5_mortality),
    n = n()
  )
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          This comparison helps us investigate whether the observations with
          missing skilled birth attendance figures differ from those with
          available figures.
          Any difference does not establish why the data is missing, or tell us
          what the missing values would have been.
        </p>

        <p>
          Finally, if we removed every observation with missing
          <c>births_skilled</c>, we would be left with just 173 of the original
          756 country-years.
        </p>

        <p>
          If those remaining observations are concentrated in particular
          countries, income groups or years, our analysis of skilled birth
          attendance and child mortality might not represent the wider dataset.
          We should therefore investigate which observations remain before
          drawing conclusions.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-imputation-risk">
      <title>Did filling in the gaps improve our data?</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          In this chapter, we replaced missing primary school enrolment values
          with the mean of the observed values.
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Calculate the mean and standard deviation of
                <c>primary_enrolment</c> using <c>na.rm = TRUE</c>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Calculate the mean and standard deviation of
                <c>enrolment_imputed</c>.
                Which statistic changes?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Look again at <xref ref="fig-enrolment-mean-imputation"/>.
                Explain why the standard deviation changes, using what you see
                in the histograms.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Suppose someone reports the standard deviation from the imputed
                dataset, without mentioning that 219 values were estimated.
                What might a reader misunderstand?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          First, we calculate the mean and standard deviation using only the
          observed primary enrolment values.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
mean(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment,
     na.rm = TRUE)
#&gt; [1] 102.4205

sd(africa_indicators$primary_enrolment,
   na.rm = TRUE)
#&gt; [1] 19.92553
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The observed values have a mean of approximately 102.4 and a standard
          deviation of 19.9.
        </p>

        <p>
          Now we calculate the same statistics after replacing all missing
          values with the observed mean.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
mean(enrolment_imputed)
#&gt; [1] 102.4205

sd(enrolment_imputed)
#&gt; [1] 16.78877
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The mean remains unchanged, but the standard deviation decreases from
          approximately 19.9 to 16.8.
        </p>

        <p>
          The mean stays the same because every missing value was replaced with
          the observed mean.
          Adding more values equal to the mean does not change the mean.
        </p>

        <p>
          However, the standard deviation decreases because we have added 219
          values that are all exactly equal to the mean.
          These values introduce no additional spread around the mean.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is visible in <xref ref="fig-enrolment-mean-imputation"/>.
          The second histogram contains a large concentration of values around
          102.4 that was not present in the original observed data.
        </p>

        <p>
          If someone reported the new standard deviation without explaining the
          imputation, a reader might conclude that primary enrolment rates vary
          less between country-years than the observed data suggests.
        </p>

        <p>
          This illustrates an important limitation of mean imputation.
          Although it produces a complete column, it can make the data appear
          less variable without providing any new information about the missing
          observations.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-wald-survivorship">
      <title>The Bombers That Didn't Come Back</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          During the Second World War, the US military looked at bombers coming
          back from missions over Germany, and recorded where each one had been
          hit.
          The damage wasn't evenly spread.
          There was far more of it on the wings and fuselage than on the
          engines.
          The obvious recommendation followed straight away: reinforce the wings
          and fuselage, since that's clearly where the bullets go.
        </p>

        <p>
          The mathematician Abraham Wald was working with a team the US military
          had put together to bring statistics to exactly this kind of problem,
          and he disagreed.
          The damage counts were almost certainly accurate.
          His objection was about which planes had been counted at all.
        </p>

        <p>
          Every bomber in that dataset had survived its mission and made it
          home.
          A bomber that was hit badly enough in the engine didn't come back to
          be inspected, photographed, and added to the tally.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Whose damage is missing from this dataset, and why?
            Think about what has to be true of a bomber for it to end up
            recorded at all.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            The planes missing from the dataset are the ones that were shot
            down, or damaged so badly that they never made it back to base.
            A plane only gets inspected and added to the tally if it survives
            the mission and lands, so the dataset only ever tells you about
            <q>damage a plane can survive</q>, and never about
            <q>damage that
            brings a plane down</q>.
            Whether a plane appears in the data at all depends directly on how
            badly it was hit, and where.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-b">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The engines show very little damage in the data.
            Give two different explanations for that pattern, one where the
            engines really were rarely hit, and one where they were hit just as
            often as anywhere else.
            What would have to be true of the missing planes for each
            explanation to be correct?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            <term>Explanation one: engines really were rarely hit.</term> This
            would be true if enemy fire, for whatever reason, tended to miss the
            engines and land on the wings and fuselage instead, perhaps because
            of the plane's shape, flight path, or the positioning of gunners.
            Under this explanation, the missing planes (the ones that didn't
            come back) would show roughly the same pattern as the surviving
            planes: mostly wing and fuselage hits, occasionally an engine hit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <term>Explanation two: engines were hit just as often, but rarely
      survived.</term>
            This would be true if hits were spread fairly evenly across the
            whole aircraft, but a hit to the engine was usually fatal to the
            mission, while a hit to the wing or fuselage usually wasn't.
            Under this explanation, the missing planes would show a very
            different pattern from the surviving planes: heavily concentrated on
            the engines, which is exactly why none of them are in the data to
            show it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The dataset alone can't distinguish between these two explanations,
            because it only contains the survivors either way.
            Wald's contribution was recognising that the second explanation was
            far more plausible on engineering grounds, and building a method
            that assumed it.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-c">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose you simply excluded any plane you had no data for, in this
            case, this is every plane that never returned, and analysed only the
            bombers that came back.
            Would this be safe?
            Compare this to a case where a few data logs were lost to a filing
            error unrelated to how damaged the plane was.
            Why does one gap sink the analysis and the other doesn't?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            It would not be safe, and it's already exactly what happened, since
            the data was never gathered on the planes that didn't return in the
            first place.
            The problem is that whether a plane is missing is tied directly to
            how much damage it took, and specifically where.
            Missing planes aren't a random subset of all planes.
            They're the worst-damaged ones, concentrated in one part of the
            aircraft.
            Removing them (or, as happened, never having them) removes exactly
            the evidence you'd need to see the true pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that to a handful of paperwork logs lost in a filing error.
            If which logs get lost has nothing to do with where the plane was
            hit, some records for lightly-damaged planes and some for
            heavily-damaged planes will disappear at roughly the same rate.
            The planes you can still see remain a fair picture of all planes,
            just a slightly smaller one.
            One kind of gap changes what conclusion you'd draw.
            The other just costs you a bit of precision.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-wald-d">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Wald's actual recommendation was to reinforce the sections that were
            <em>least</em> represented in the damage data, not the most.
            Using your answer to <xref ref="ex-wald-a"/>, explain why that's the
            opposite conclusion from the obvious one, and why it's the correct
            one.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            The obvious recommendation treats heavy damage in the data as a sign
            of a weak spot:
            <q>we see a lot of wing damage, so wings must be
            vulnerable, so let's reinforce them.</q>
            But because the data only contains survivors, lots of visible damage
            to a section is actually evidence that the section can take a hit
            and keep flying.
            It's a sign of strength.
          </p>

          <p>
            A section that's barely damaged in the data, like the engines, isn't
            necessarily a section that rarely gets hit.
            Given that hits are spread fairly evenly over the whole airframe,
            its absence from the data is far better explained by planes hit
            there not surviving to be counted.
            So the section with the least visible damage is the one hiding the
            planes you never got to see, which is exactly the section that most
            needs reinforcing.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    <!-- R: missing values -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-clean-missing-values">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The Kenya Road Accidents database contains missing values in several
          columns.
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Write R code to count the number of missing values in each
                column.
                Which column has the most missing values?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                The <c>AGE</c> column has both true missing values and coded
                values like <c>A</c> and <c>J</c>.
                After converting <c>AGE</c> to numeric, how many <c>NA</c> s
                result?
                Are all of these genuinely unknown ages, or are some of them
                something else?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Explain in your own words the difference between a value that is
                missing and a value that is unknown.
                Does that distinction matter for this dataset?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          We can count the missing values in each column using <c>is.na()</c>
          and <c>colSums()</c>.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
colSums(is.na(kenya_accidents_2016))
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The column with the largest count has the most values recorded as
          <c>NA</c>.
          However, this only identifies values that R already recognises as
          missing.
        </p>

        <p>
          The <c>AGE</c> column illustrates why we need to be careful.
          It contains numerical ages as well as codes such as <c>A</c> and
          <c>J</c>.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can investigate what happens when we convert this column to
          numeric.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
age_numeric &lt;- suppressWarnings(
  as.numeric(kenya_accidents_2016$AGE)
)

sum(is.na(age_numeric))
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          Some values become <c>NA</c> because they were already missing.
          Others become <c>NA</c> because R cannot convert a code such as
          <c>A</c> or <c>J</c> into a number.
        </p>

        <p>
          We can investigate which original values could not be converted.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
kenya_accidents_2016 %&gt;%
  mutate(age_numeric = suppressWarnings(
    as.numeric(AGE)
  )) %&gt;%
  filter(is.na(age_numeric)) %&gt;%
  count(AGE, sort = TRUE)
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          This is important because not all of these values mean the same thing.
          A genuinely unknown age is different from a code that describes an age
          category.
        </p>

        <p>
          For example, if <c>A</c> represents an adult and <c>J</c> represents a
          juvenile, those records still contain information about age, even
          though they do not give an exact numerical age.
          We should confirm the meanings of these codes from the data
          documentation before using them.
        </p>

        <p>
          Simply converting the column to numeric would discard that
          information.
        </p>

        <p>
          A missing value means that no usable value is recorded for the
          variable in its current form.
          An unknown value means that the information itself is not known.
          These are not always the same thing.
        </p>

        <p>
          In this dataset, a value may appear missing after conversion even
          though the original record contains useful information.
          This is why we should inspect unusual values before deciding how to
          handle them.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-challenger-missing">
      <title>The Launches That Weren't on the Chart</title>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="ex-challenger"/> we looked at the night before the
          Challenger launch in January 1986.
          Engineers at Morton Thiokol, the company that built the rocket
          boosters, argued the launch should be delayed because the O-ring seals
          could fail in the cold.
          NASA went ahead, and all seven crew members were killed.
        </p>

        <p>
          Edward Tufte's Challenger graph is a famous case study in data
          visualisation.
          It shows how a scatter plot of O-ring damage against launch
          temperature could have made the dangers of the launch really clear.
          In his book <em>Visual Explanations</em> (1997), Tufte argued that the
          disaster was partly a failure of data communication: the engineers had
          the evidence, but it wasn't shown in a way that let anyone see the
          pattern.
        </p>

        <p>
          Tufte's argument, and our exercise, focused on how the data was shown.
          But there's another problem with the charts the engineers used, and
          it's the same kind of problem as Wald's bombers.
          When they looked at whether temperature mattered, they only included
          launches where O-ring damage had been found.
          The launches with no damage at all weren't there.
        </p>

        <p>
          We use the same <c>orings</c> data from the <c>DAAG</c> package.
          Each row is one launch before Challenger, <c>Temperature</c> is the
          launch temperature in degrees Fahrenheit, and <c>Total</c> is the
          number of O-rings showing damage.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
            library(DAAG)
            library(dplyr)
            library(ggplot2)
          </code>
        </program>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Filter <c>orings</c> down to the launches where <c>Total &gt; 0</c>,
            which is what the engineers' charts showed.
            How many launches are in the full data, and how many are left after
            filtering?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <program language="r">
            <code>
              orings_damaged &lt;- orings %&gt;%
                filter(Total &gt; 0)

              nrow(orings)
              nrow(orings_damaged)
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            Most of the launches disappear.
            Only the minority with some damage are left, so any chart built from
            <c>orings_damaged</c> is describing a much smaller, and very
            particular, group of launches.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-b">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Plot <c>Temperature</c> against <c>Total</c> twice: once for all
            launches, and once for the damaged launches only.
            Add the forecast temperature for the Challenger launch (26 to 29°F)
            as a shaded region, as you did in <xref ref="ex-challenger-b"/>.
            How does the relationship between temperature and damage change when
            the zero-damage launches are left out?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <program language="r">
            <code>
              ggplot(orings_damaged, aes(x = Temperature, y = Total)) +
                geom_point() +
                xlim(25, 85) +
                ylim(0, 6) +
                annotate("rect",
                         xmin = 26, xmax = 29,
                         ymin = 0,  ymax = 6,
                         alpha = 0.3, fill = "red") +
                annotate("text",
                         x = 33, y = 3,
                         label = "Forecast\ntemperature\n26 to 29°F",
                         hjust = 0) +
                labs(x = "Temperature (°F)",
                     y = "Total O-ring damage",
                     title = "O-ring damage by launch temperature",
                     subtitle = "Launches with damage only: zero-damage launches excluded") +
                theme_bw()
            </code>
          </program>

          <p>
            For the full data, swap <c>orings_damaged</c> for <c>orings</c> and
            change the subtitle.
          </p>

          <p>
            With every launch included, the pattern is clear: the cold launches
            all had damage, and nearly all the damage-free launches happened at
            warmer temperatures.
            Take the zero-damage launches away and that pattern really fades.
            What's left is damage scattered across a range of temperatures, and
            it looks like damage can happen whether it's cold or warm.
            The launches that were left out were the ones that showed warm
            launches tend to go fine, and that was the evidence that cold was
            the problem.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-c">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What decided whether a launch ended up on the chart?
            Were the launches that got left out just a random handful, or was
            there something about them that made them go missing?
            How does that compare to Wald's bombers?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            It wasn't random at all.
            Whether a launch was included depended on its value of <c>Total</c>:
            launches with zero damage were dropped, and launches with some
            damage were kept.
            So the launches went missing because of the very thing we were
            trying to measure.
            That's the same structure as the bombers, where whether a plane was
            in the data depended on how badly it had been hit.
            And just like the bombers, it means the data that's left gives us a
            distorted picture, not just a smaller one.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-challenger-missing-d">
        <statement>
          <p>
            No amount of extra data
            collected the same way could fix Wald's problem.
            Is the Challenger case the same?
            What would it have taken to fix it?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
          <p>
            No, and this is what makes the Challenger case so hard to look at.
            Wald's missing planes were lost over enemy territory, and nobody
            could ever inspect them.
            The zero-damage launches were never missing from the records.
            The engineers had that data; it just didn't make it onto the chart.
            The fix didn't need new data or a clever model of the missingness.
            It needed someone to ask
            <q>which launches are we not looking at, and why?</q>
          </p>

          <p>
            Missing data isn't only something that happens to us during data
            collection.
            We can also create it ourselves, with a filter or a decision about
            what's <q>relevant</q>, and it can do just as much damage.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <conclusion>
        <p>
          <term>Sources:</term> Tufte, E.R.
          (1997).
          <em>Visual Explanations: Images and Quantities, Evidence and
          Narrative.</em>
          Graphics Press.
          Data from the <c>DAAG</c> R package.
          Case study adapted from Helveston, J.P.
          (2019), George Washington University,
          <url href="https://p4a.seas.gwu.edu/2019-Fall/challengerExplosion.html"/>,
          CC BY 4.0.
        </p>
      </conclusion>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>